[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How it was checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - C: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `c_src/build/driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `translation/target/release/driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `translation/tests/differential.rs` runs both binaries as subprocesses and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   compares stdout, stderr and exit status (including death by signal). The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   input space is exhaustive: all 256 possible byte values, plus empty input,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   plus non-byte-valued conditions (multi-byte stdin, arguments, failing stdin,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   broken/closed/file stdout).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status on a broken stdout pipe (fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | C | Rust (before fix) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdout with no reader | killed by `SIGPIPE`, status 141 | exited 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`, so writes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to a pipe with no reader return `EPIPE` instead of terminating the process. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation discarded write errors (correctly, mirroring C's stdio, which does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not report them), so the process ran to a clean exit. The C program inherits the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m default `SIGPIPE` disposition and dies from signal 13.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `reset_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the top of `main`. `signal` is declared in a local `extern "C"` block; libc is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already linked on the gnu targets, so no new dependency was added.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Test.** `broken_stdout_pipe_exit_status`. It hands the child a stdout pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whose read end is closed and asserts both programs die from signal 13. Two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m details make it deterministic rather than racy:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the pipe is created with `pipe2(O_CLOEXEC)`. With plain `pipe()` the child
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   inherits the read end of its own stdout pipe, keeps a reader alive, and never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   sees a broken pipe — the first version of this test failed for exactly that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reason and reported C exiting 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the read end is closed while the child is still blocked reading stdin, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pipe is guaranteed broken before the child's first write.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours verified as already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places the translation could plausibly have diverged. Each was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed against the C binary, not reasoned about in the abstract.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`is*` return the masked class bit, not `1`.** glibc's `ctype.h` defines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `isalpha(c)` as a `__ctype_b` table lookup masked with `_ISalpha`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `printf("%d", isalpha('a'))` prints `1024`. The translation reproduces every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `_ISbit` value: upper 256, lower 512, alpha 1024, digit 2048, xdigit 4096,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   space 8192, print 16384, graph 32768, blank 1, cntrl 2, punct 4, alnum 8.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   A run on `'a'` prints `alphabetic: 1024`, `graphical: 32768`, `alphanumeric: 8`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Bytes `0x80`–`0xFF`.** `char` is signed here, so these become negative
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   indices into `__ctype_b`. glibc's table covers `-128..=255` and the C locale
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   leaves the high half all-zero with identity case mapping, so every class
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reports `0`. Signedness turns out to be unobservable: the unsigned reading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (indices 128–255) gives the same answers.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **EOF.** `char c = getchar()` truncates `EOF` to `(char)-1`, which indexes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   entry for `0xFF`: all classes `0`, and `%c` of `tolower(-1)` emits a raw
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0xFF` byte. Empty stdin, a closed fd 0, and a directory as stdin all take
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   this path identically in both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%c` writes a raw byte.** `printf("%c", ...)` converts to `unsigned char`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and writes one byte, never UTF-8. For input `0xFF` the last line is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `to upper: \xff\n` and stdout is not valid UTF-8;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `high_byte_output_is_raw_not_utf8` asserts this so the Rust side cannot
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   silently start encoding `char` values.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Only the first byte is consumed.** A leading `\n` is returned as data rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   than skipped, and trailing input is ignored (checked up to 100 000 bytes).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Arguments are ignored.** `main` takes no parameters.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Closed stdout (fd 1 closed).** Writes fail with `EBADF`; C's stdio swallows
